---

<LessonPage page={page}>
<main class="wrap" data-core-duration="50–60 minutes" data-extension-duration="15–20 minutes">
  <p class="kicker"><span class="dot"></span> Vocabulary · Lesson {page.sequence}</p>
  <h1>Town &amp; directions: <em>build a route someone can follow</em></h1>
  <p class="standfirst">Good directions connect a known start point to a destination. Keep the steps in order, name the exact turn point, use landmarks as checks, and finish with the destination’s side or location relationship.</p>
  <div class="meta-row">
    <span class="tag">50–60 min core</span>
    <span class="tag">start · move · turn · check · arrive</span>
    <span class="tag">ask · confirm · repair</span>
    <span class="tag">15–20 min listening and retrieval</span>
  </div>

  <div class="lesson-flow" aria-label="Lesson flow">
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Notice</b><span>Find six route jobs in one set of directions.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Discover</b><span>Separate movement from location and viewpoint.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Build</b><span>Order turns, blocks, landmarks, sides, and checks.</span></div>
    <div class="flow-step"><b>Use</b><span>Guide a fictional visitor and repair one misunderstanding.</span></div>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line"><b>Can-do:</b> By the end, you can ask for directions politely; give an ordered walking route from a clear start point; distinguish movement from location; name an exact turn point and landmark; locate the destination from the traveler’s viewpoint; and confirm or repair a misunderstood step.</p>

  <h2>Two-minute retrieval</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You retrieved a nearby-place question, street location, and imperative instruction.">
    <h3>Three checks from earlier lessons</h3>
    <p class="choice-hint">Use a there-is question, a street preposition, and a base-verb imperative.</p>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Earlier lesson choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Is there">Is there</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="There is">There is</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on">on</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="in">in</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Open">Open</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Opens">Opens</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The visitor asks whether one pharmacy exists nearby: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Is there" data-hint="Begin the singular existence question with Is there." data-fix="Is there a pharmacy near here?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> a pharmacy near here?</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>Main Street is the street address: The pharmacy is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on" data-hint="Use on before a street name in this location phrase." data-fix="The pharmacy is on Main Street." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Main Street.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The speaker gives a direct instruction with no subject: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Open" data-hint="An affirmative imperative begins with the base verb Open." data-fix="Open the map, please." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the map, please.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice six jobs in one complete route</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Cedar Square visitor route</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">“<strong>Start outside Cedar Station</strong>, facing Main Street. <strong>Walk along Main Street for two blocks</strong>. <strong>Turn left at the second traffic light onto Pine Avenue</strong>. <strong>Go past the public library</strong>. The museum is <strong>on your right, across from the park</strong>. If you reach the hospital, <strong>you’ve gone one block too far</strong>.”</span></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Which phrase fixes the start point and the traveler’s viewpoint?</li>
      <li>Which phrases move the traveler, identify the turn point, and provide a landmark check?</li>
      <li>Which phrase locates the destination, and which one signals that the traveler passed it?</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You identified the start viewpoint, movement, turn point, passed landmark, destination location, and too-far check.">
    <h3>Discovery check: identify each route job</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Route job choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="start point and viewpoint">start point and viewpoint</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="forward movement">forward movement</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="exact turn point">exact turn point</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="passed landmark">passed landmark</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="destination location">destination location</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="too-far check">too-far check</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span><span class="ex">Start outside Cedar Station, facing Main Street</span> establishes the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="start point and viewpoint" data-hint="The phrase states where the traveler begins and which way the traveler faces." data-fix="Outside Cedar Station facing Main Street is the start point and viewpoint." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span><span class="ex">Walk along Main Street for two blocks</span> gives the route’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="forward movement" data-hint="Walk along tells the traveler to continue following Main Street." data-fix="Walk along Main Street for two blocks gives the forward movement." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span><span class="ex">At the second traffic light</span> identifies the <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="exact turn point" data-hint="The ordinal and landmark identify where the left turn happens." data-fix="The second traffic light is the exact turn point." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span><span class="ex">Go past the public library</span> uses the library as a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="passed landmark" data-hint="The traveler continues beyond the library instead of turning there." data-fix="The public library is a passed landmark." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span><span class="ex">On your right, across from the park</span> gives the museum’s <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="destination location" data-hint="The side and relationship tell the traveler where the museum is." data-fix="On your right across from the park is the destination location." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span><span class="ex">If you reach the hospital</span> means the traveler has passed the museum, so the hospital is a <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="too-far check" data-hint="This landmark appears after the destination and signals an overshoot." data-fix="The hospital is the too-far check." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Get to the root: build from viewpoint to destination</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Left</span> and <span class="ex">right</span> belong to the traveler’s current viewpoint. A usable route therefore begins at a shared start point, keeps the steps in order, and updates the viewpoint after each turn.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>route stage</th><th>language job</th><th>example</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1. establish</td><td>name the start point and optional facing direction</td><td><span class="ex">Start outside the station, facing Main Street.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2. move</td><td>follow a street or cover a distance</td><td><span class="ex">Walk along Main Street for two blocks.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>3. change direction</td><td>name the side, exact point, and new street</td><td><span class="ex">Turn left at the second light onto Pine Avenue.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>4. verify</td><td>pass or notice a landmark</td><td><span class="ex">Go past the public library.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>5. arrive</td><td>give the destination side and relationship</td><td><span class="ex">It’s on your right, across from the park.</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>6. recover</td><td>name a too-far landmark or repair</td><td><span class="ex">If you reach the hospital, go back one block.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="note-line">A route is not a bag of interchangeable commands. Changing the order, turn point, or traveler’s viewpoint can change the destination.</p>

  <h2>Choose a movement or a location relationship</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>movement instruction</th><th>meaning</th><th>location relationship</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">walk along Oak Street</span></td><td>follow the street</td><td><span class="ex">on Oak Street</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">go straight for two blocks</span></td><td>continue without turning</td><td><span class="ex">at the end of the block</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">turn right at the light</span></td><td>change direction at that point</td><td><span class="ex">on your right</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">turn onto Pine Avenue</span></td><td>enter and follow the named street</td><td><span class="ex">on the corner of Pine and Oak</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">go past the bank</span></td><td>continue beyond the bank</td><td><span class="ex">next to the bank</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><span class="ex">cross Pine Avenue</span></td><td>move from one side to the other</td><td><span class="ex">across from the park</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p><span class="ex">Past</span> is the correct word after <span class="ex">go</span>: <span class="ex">Go past the library.</span> <span class="ex">Passed</span> is the past-tense verb: <span class="ex">We passed the library five minutes ago.</span> <span class="ex">Cross the street</span> is movement, while <span class="ex">across from the park</span> is a location relationship.</p>
  <p>A city <span class="ex">block</span> is a section of street between neighboring cross streets. Its length varies, and the count depends on the stated start point. When exact distance matters, add time or distance: <span class="ex">about a five-minute walk</span> or <span class="ex">about 400 yards</span>.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You selected movement, turn, crossing, passed-landmark, destination-side, and location-relation chunks from the route context.">
    <h3>Drill 1: move or locate?</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Movement and location choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="walk along">walk along</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="turn onto">turn onto</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="go past">go past</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="cross">cross</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on your left">on your left</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="across from">across from</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The traveler should follow Oak Street without leaving it: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="walk along" data-hint="Walk along means follow the named street." data-fix="Walk along Oak Street for one block." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Oak Street for one block.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>At the light, the traveler should enter Pine Avenue and follow it: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="turn onto" data-hint="Turn onto names the new street entered after the turn." data-fix="Turn onto Pine Avenue at the traffic light." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Pine Avenue at the traffic light.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The library is a check, but the traveler must continue beyond it: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="go past" data-hint="Go past means continue beyond the landmark." data-fix="Go past the public library and keep walking." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the public library and keep walking.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The traveler must move from one side of Pine Avenue to the other at the marked crosswalk: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="cross" data-hint="Cross names movement from one side of a street to the other." data-fix="Cross Pine Avenue at the marked crosswalk when the signal allows." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> Pine Avenue at the marked crosswalk when the signal allows.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>After the final turn, the museum is on the traveler’s left side: The museum is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on your left" data-hint="On your left locates the destination from the traveler’s current viewpoint." data-fix="The museum is on your left after the final turn." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> after the final turn.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The museum and park face each other on opposite sides of the street: The museum is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="across from" data-hint="Across from describes two places on opposite sides." data-fix="The museum is across from the park." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> the park.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Ask, check, and repair directions politely</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p><span class="ex">Excuse me, where is the museum?</span> and <span class="ex">Excuse me, how do I get to the museum?</span> are both natural polite requests. The first asks for location; the second directly asks for a route. A longer option is <span class="ex">Excuse me, could you tell me how to get to the museum?</span></p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>communication need</th><th>useful question</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>check the route</td><td><span class="ex">Is this the way to the museum?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>check distance</td><td><span class="ex">How far is it? Is it within walking distance?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>request repetition</td><td><span class="ex">Could you repeat that more slowly, please?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>check one detail</td><td><span class="ex">Did you say the first or second traffic light?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>confirm a step</td><td><span class="ex">So I turn left at the bank, correct?</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>repair a detail</td><td><span class="ex">Pine Avenue, not Pine Street.</span></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>When a detail matters, repeating the route is not rude. It is good communication. Confirm the start, ordinal number, street name, side, or landmark before continuing.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You matched location, route, distance, repetition, detail confirmation, and route repair to the speaker’s exact need.">
    <h3>Drill 2: choose the useful question or repair</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Direction clarification choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Excuse me, where is the museum?">Excuse me, where is the museum?</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Excuse me, how do I get to the museum?">Excuse me, how do I get to the museum?</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="How far is it?">How far is it?</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Could you repeat that more slowly, please?">Could you repeat that more slowly, please?</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Did you say the first or second light?">Did you say the first or second light?</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Pine Avenue, not Pine Street.">Pine Avenue, not Pine Street.</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The visitor wants only the place’s location, not a full route: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Excuse me, where is the museum?" data-hint="Where is asks directly for the location and Excuse me makes the request polite." data-fix="Excuse me, where is the museum?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The visitor wants step-by-step directions from the current location: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Excuse me, how do I get to the museum?" data-hint="How do I get to asks for the route." data-fix="Excuse me, how do I get to the museum?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>The visitor needs to know whether the trip is short or long: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="How far is it?" data-hint="How far asks about distance." data-fix="How far is it? It’s about a five-minute walk." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The speaker’s route was too fast to understand: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Could you repeat that more slowly, please?" data-hint="Request slower repetition instead of pretending the route was clear." data-fix="Could you repeat that more slowly, please?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The visitor heard the landmark but is unsure about its ordinal number: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Did you say the first or second light?" data-hint="Ask a focused either-or question about the uncertain number." data-fix="Did you say the first or second light?" data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>The listener repeats the destination street as Pine Street, but the correct name is Pine Avenue: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Pine Avenue, not Pine Street." data-hint="Put contrastive stress on the corrected street type." data-fix="Pine Avenue, not Pine Street." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button></span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Keep route order and reference precise</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>Connect steps with <span class="ex">first, then, after that</span>, and <span class="ex">finally</span>. A pronoun such as <span class="ex">it</span> is useful only when its reference is clear: <span class="ex">Go past the bank. The museum is next to it.</span> When two landmarks could be <span class="ex">it</span>, repeat the place name.</p>
  <p>Use <span class="ex">at</span> for the turn point and <span class="ex">onto</span> for the street entered: <span class="ex">Turn right at the bank onto Cedar Street.</span> Use <span class="ex">on the corner of A and B</span> to locate a building at an intersection, but <span class="ex">turn at the corner</span> to name the movement point.</p>

  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You preserved start point, ordered connector, turn point, entered street, clear landmark reference, and final destination side.">
    <h3>Drill 3: make the route executable</h3>
    <div class="choice-bank" aria-label="Route precision choices"><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Start outside Cedar Station">Start outside Cedar Station</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="Then">Then</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="at the second light">at the second light</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="onto Pine Avenue">onto Pine Avenue</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="the library">the library</button><button class="choice-option" data-choice-option="on your right">on your right</button></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">1</span><span>The route must establish the shared beginning before any left or right instruction: <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Start outside Cedar Station" data-hint="Name the exact start point before giving viewpoint-dependent turns." data-fix="Start outside Cedar Station, facing Main Street." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>, facing Main Street.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">2</span><span>The traveler walks two blocks before the next step, so introduce that later action with <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="Then" data-hint="Then marks the next event in the ordered route." data-fix="Walk two blocks. Then turn left." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> turn left.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">3</span><span>There are two traffic lights, and the turn happens at number two: Turn left <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="at the second light" data-hint="At introduces the exact turn point; second distinguishes it from the first light." data-fix="Turn left at the second light." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">4</span><span>The left turn enters and follows Pine Avenue: Turn left <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="onto Pine Avenue" data-hint="Onto names the new street entered by the turn." data-fix="Turn left onto Pine Avenue." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button>.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">5</span><span>The route mentioned a bank and library, so repeat the intended landmark instead of using ambiguous <span class="ex">it</span>: Go past <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="the library" data-hint="Repeat the library because two possible landmarks were mentioned." data-fix="Go past the library and keep walking." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> and keep walking.</span></div>
    <div class="q"><span class="n">6</span><span>After the final turn, the destination appears on the traveler’s right side: The museum is <button class="choice-gap" data-choice-gap data-answer="on your right" data-hint="On your right uses the traveler’s updated viewpoint after the turn." data-fix="The museum is on your right after the final turn." data-placeholder="choose">choose</button> after the final turn.</span></div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-choices>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-choices>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <h3>Sound natural: stress the decision points and chunk the route</h3>
    <p>The listener needs movement, side, number, street, and landmark. Stress those words: <span class="ex"><strong>TURN LEFT</strong> at the <strong>SECOND LIGHT</strong>. Go past the <strong>LIBRARY</strong>. It’s on your <strong>RIGHT</strong>.</span></p>
    <p><span class="ex">Straight /streɪt/</span> is one syllable. Keep the opening /str/ together and do not pronounce <span class="ex">gh</span>. Finish the consonants in <span class="ex">left /lɛft/</span> and <span class="ex">right /raɪt/</span> so the two sides remain distinct.</p>
    <p>In connected U.S. speech, <span class="ex">How do I get to the museum?</span> often has weak <span class="ex">do</span> and <span class="ex">to</span>: /haʊ də aɪ ɡɛt tə ðə mjuˈziəm/. The destination remains strong. <span class="ex">Across</span> stresses its second syllable: /əˈkrɔs/.</p>
    <p>Pause at route boundaries instead of after every word: <span class="ex">Start at the station | walk two blocks | turn left at the second light | and the museum is on your right.</span> Use contrastive stress to repair details: <span class="ex">the <strong>SECOND</strong> light, not the first</span>.</p>
  </div>

  <section data-lesson-extension="Tutor-read start point, turn number, entered street, passed landmark, destination location, and corrected detail listening check">
    <h2>Listen for the route decisions</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p class="note-line"><b>Tutor-read listening:</b> Keep the transcript closed. Read each item once naturally and once more after the learner chooses. Preserve normal reductions, but keep the decision words clear.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You heard the start point, turn number, entered street, passed landmark, destination location, and corrected detail.">
      <h3>Six route-message decisions</h3>
      <div class="quiz-list">
        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="outside Cedar Station" data-hint="Listen for the place after Start." data-fix="The route starts outside Cedar Station."><p class="quiz-question">1. Where does the route begin?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="outside Cedar Station">outside Cedar Station</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="inside the public library">inside the public library</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="across from the hospital">across from the hospital</button></div></div>
