      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>3</b> Continue beyond the bank. Use <span class="ex">go past</span>.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Go past the bank and keep walking.</strong></p></div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>4</b> The park is on the opposite side from the museum. Locate the park.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>The park is across from the museum.</strong></p></div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>5</b> You did not hear whether the turn is at light one or two. Ask a focused question.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Did you say the first or second traffic light?</strong></p></div>
      <div class="transform-item" data-transform-item><p class="transform-cue"><b>6</b> The listener says Pine Street, but the correct place is Pine Avenue. Repair it.</p><button class="lesson-btn" data-reveal-transform>Reveal after you answer</button><p class="transform-answer" data-transform-answer><strong>Pine Avenue, not Pine Street.</strong></p></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Communicate: guide a fictional visitor</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to disclose your real home, current location, workplace, school, commute, daily route, neighborhood, favorite places, travel plans, safety habits, schedule, or another person’s address. Use Cedar Square, invent a neutral town, or share personal information only when you freely choose.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Prepare the fictional map:</b> Choose a station or bus stop, two named streets, two turn points, two landmarks, a destination, and one too-far landmark. Decide the traveler’s starting viewpoint.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Final production: give a 45–60 second fictional visitor route.</b> Establish the start, give at least four ordered movement steps, name an exact turn point and street entered, use one passed landmark, locate the destination by side and relationship, and add a too-far check.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Meaning check:</b> The listener repeats the route back and intentionally changes one ordinal, side, street type, or landmark. Correct only that detail with contrastive stress, then confirm the repaired route.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Change the need:</b> Ask for location, a complete route, distance, slower repetition, and one detail confirmation. Choose the shortest question that matches each need.</div>

